Dear Ale, and anyone else paying attention to this thread,

You asked a couple of questions earlier about trajectory analysis and limitations with using Species as a term in a model for extended PGLS.  Regarding the latter question, I felt provoked to follow-up on what I mentioned yesterday, that the results were different using different values of delta, the weight-scaling parameter for the covariance matrix.  I can confirm that this is really only an issue for the Species effect, which is a weird term to be testing when accounting for species relatedness.  Nevertheless, one might want to test for an adaptive radiation, for example, so it is not too weird to consider.  Regardless, I had not done something like create a gradient of delta to see how effects change.  (I had tuned the parameter before to rid myself of the weird Species effect, but I had not considered what might happen as a pattern to all effects.)  Now I have done that and I think it might be helpful.  Rather than just email the result, I decided to record it for posterity in blog form <https://mlcollyer.github.io/collyerblog/posts/2026-11-6-Extended-PGLS/>.  Hopefully this helps offer some guidance.  The short version is that only the species effect is volatile and only when delta is near 0.  Increasing delta causes the species effect to stabilize.  All other effects remain consistent.

Regarding the first question, I glossed over the note you made that some species are poorly sampled.  This could have some effect for trajectory analysis, if the RRPP is restricted to species blocks.  The good news is you can compare trajectories among well-sampled species without the poorly sampled species negatively impacting your inferences.  The within-species RRPP strategy makes the test robust for groups with ample replication.

Speaking of blogs, I decided to make them cool again by dedicating some space for one on my website.  There are a few other posts about things like whether to adjust P-values for posthoc tests, how awesome gpagen is, and how not all distance-based ANOVAs for multivariate data are equal (spoiler alert, our functions are pretty fast).  I am just getting started but my hope is to have a more helpful reference bank than just the google group archives.

All the best,
Mike

